# Obsidian CS Knowledge Base — Note Conventions

This vault belongs to Akash Singh. Full conventions are in `CONVENTIONS.md`.
Always read that file first when writing or converting notes.

## Quick Reference

**Folder rules:** DSA = one note per topic. LLD = one note per design problem.
HLD = one subfolder per system with its own `.md` inside.

**Every note starts with:**
```
---
tags: [dsa/topic, status/draft]
created: YYYY-MM-DD
---
# Title
> [!abstract] One paragraph: what this is and why it matters
```

**Callouts:** `[!abstract]` open, `[!tip]` key insight, `[!warning]` gotcha,
`[!danger]` failure mode, `[!info]` context, `[!example]` worked example.

**Diagrams:** Always Mermaid first (`flowchart TD` default). Excalidraw only when
structure genuinely cannot be expressed as text.

**DSA notes must have:** pattern name, when-to-use signal, plain-English idea,
code block, complexity table (Time + Space), key variants, classic problems.

**HLD notes must have:** requirements (functional + non-functional), scale estimates
table, architecture Mermaid diagram, component deep dives, database choices table,
failure modes table, key design decisions.

**LLD notes must have:** requirements, class diagram (Mermaid `classDiagram`),
key methods, design patterns used, edge cases.

## Converting Handwritten Notes

User will provide a PDF exported from OneNote (pages are images of freehand writing).
Reconstruct as a properly structured `.md` file following these conventions.
Recreate drawn diagrams as Mermaid. One OneNote section = one `.md` file.

## Style Rules

- `==highlight==` only for the single most critical fact on a line
- Link related concepts: `[[Consistent Hashing]]`, `[[CAP Theorem]]`
- Keep Mermaid node labels short (2–4 words, `\n` for breaks)
- Complexity values always in backticks: `O(log n)`
- No filler sentences. Every line earns its place.
